const notes=[
{id:"BG001",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M101",fact:"For each of the finite coordinate families B_j(·,t0) and T_j(·,t0), the pointwise sum over 1≤j≤n is dx-integrable on V; this also holds when n=0.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 supplies integrability of each B_j and T_j section."},{location:"/parameters/0",reason:"n is a natural number, so the coordinate index set is finite."},{location:"/definitions/0/definition",reason:"V carries the stated Lebesgue measure."}],justification:"The probed MeasureTheory.integrable_finsetSum applies with α=V, μ=dx, ε'=ℝ, the finite coordinate set, and f(j,x)=B_j(x,t0) or T_j(x,t0). ℝ has the required seminormed commutative additive and continuous-addition structures. This is the routine finite-sum integrability implicit in S002 and S003, not a new analytic estimate."},
{id:"BG002",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M102",fact:"The sections 2A, 2∑_j B_j, and −∑_j T_j at t0 are dx-integrable whenever their unscaled sections are; the same applies to 2(A−∑_j T_j) after its integrability is established.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 gives integrability of A and the component sections."},{location:"/inference/argument/1/text",reason:"Only the constant scalar 2 and finite sums are used here."},{location:"/inference/argument/2/text",reason:"The combination uses scalar −1 and then 2."}],justification:"The probed Integrable.smul applies to real-valued functions on V with real constants 2 and −1. The usual real scalar action fixes zero and is bounded (|cf|=|c||f|); it has the required normed-group structures. BG001 supplies the sum integrability. This discharges the routine scalar-integrability conditions of M013 without changing the proof."},
{id:"BG003",at:"/inference/argument/2/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M103",fact:"A(·,t0)−∑_j T_j(·,t0) is integrable, and the sum 2A(·,t0)+2∑_j B_j(·,t0) is integrable.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 gives integrability of A, B_j and T_j."},{location:"/inference/argument/1/text",reason:"The first integral decomposition uses the sum of two scalar-multiplied sections."},{location:"/inference/argument/2/text",reason:"The second combines A with the negative finite sum of T_j."}],justification:"With α=V, μ=dx and ε'=ℝ, the probed Integrable.add combines the integrable summands provided by F001 and BG001–BG002. The real codomain has continuous addition. Consequently all combined integrals in the existing linearity calculation are genuine finite integrals. No extra regularity or limit interchange is introduced."},
{id:"BG004",at:"/inference/argument/2/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M104",fact:"The dx-integral over V of the identically zero real-valued function is zero. Therefore the integral of x↦u_t(x,t0)·(u_tt(x,t0)−Δu(x,t0)) is zero.",grounds:[{location:"/inputs/0/statement",reason:"N001 says the displayed scalar integrand equals zero at every x for the fixed positive t0."},{location:"/definitions/0/definition",reason:"V and dx specify the domain and measure."},{location:"/inference/argument/0/text",reason:"t0 was fixed positive."}],justification:"N001 and function extensionality identify the integrand with the zero function; equality substitution and the probed MeasureTheory.integral_zero with α=V and G=ℝ give the asserted value. This is the omitted basic zero-integral rule, not the energy cancellation argument itself."},
{id:"BG005",at:"/inference/argument/4/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M105",fact:"A total real extension of E agreeing with E on (0,∞) has the same derivative zero at every positive time. In particular extending outside that open set does not affect any derivative used on the positive closed intervals in S005.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/3/produces/0/statement",reason:"F004 gives differentiability and derivative zero at every positive time."},{location:"/inference/argument/4/text",reason:"The extension is required to agree at every positive time; both interval endpoints are positive."},{location:"/definitions/14/type",reason:"The original E is defined on (0,∞), so this is only an extension of its domain."}],justification:"For x>0 the interval (x/2,3x/2) lies in the original domain and contains x. The extension agrees there with the original germ, so the probed HasDerivAt.congr_of_eventuallyEq transfers its derivative. All domain, codomain and scalar structures are ℝ. Such an extension is an ordinary function construction (for example value zero off the domain), not an assumed existence or a new analytical route."},
{id:"BG006",at:"/inference/argument/4/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M106",fact:"In either strict-order branch of S005, the interval [a,b] with (a,b)=(s,t) or (t,s) is a set of unique differentiability over ℝ, including at its endpoints.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/4/text",reason:"The s<t branch uses a=s,b=t; the t<s branch reverses them. Both give a<b. The equality branch uses no derivative comparison."}],justification:"The probed uniqueDiffOn_Icc has exactly the premise a<b, proved by the active branch. It gives UniqueDiffWithinAt at each point of [a,b] by unfolding UniqueDiffOn. This is a routine endpoint side condition for the supplied M005, not a replacement constancy theorem."},
{id:"BG007",at:"/inference/argument/4/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M107",fact:"For the total extension in S005, derivWithin φ [a,b] x=deriv φ x=0 at each x∈[a,b] in either strict-order branch.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/3/produces/0/statement",reason:"F004 gives the ordinary derivative zero at every positive time."},{location:"/inference/argument/4/text",reason:"Every point of either chosen interval is positive; the interval is nondegenerate and the extension preserves the positive-time derivatives."}],justification:"BG005 supplies differentiability of the extension at each interval point, and BG006 supplies UniqueDiffWithinAt there. Apply the probed fderivWithin_eq_fderiv with scalar, domain and codomain ℝ; all continuous-addition, continuous scalar action and Hausdorff conditions hold in ℝ. The read native definitions derivWithin=fderivWithin applied to 1 and deriv=fderiv applied to 1 give the claimed one-dimensional equality. Hence its norm is 0, satisfying M005 on [a,b)."},
{id:"BG008",at:"/inference/argument/4/text",kind:"mathlib",source_id:"M108",fact:"The total extension of E is differentiable on each closed positive interval used in S005, in the DifferentiableOn/within sense required by M005.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/3/produces/0/statement",reason:"F004 supplies differentiability at every positive point."},{location:"/inference/argument/4/text",reason:"The interval endpoints are positive and the total extension agrees on the positive domain."}],justification:"After the local derivative transfer in BG005, apply the probed DifferentiableAt.differentiableWithinAt at each x∈[a,b] with s=[a,b] and scalar/domain/codomain ℝ. Quantifying these within derivatives is precisely DifferentiableOn. No global differentiability outside the interval is needed."}
];store("notesDraft",notes);
for (const n of notes) text(await tools.record_background_note(n));

const checks={
applicability:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"Checked every M application against its conditional contract. A001 (M013): X=V, μ=dx, Z=ℝ, summands 2A and 2∑B; F001 and BG001–BG002 discharge both integrability premises. A002 (M014): real scalar action with c=2 on A and ∑B; real multiplication is a norm-compatible module action and commutes with real multiplication. A003/A004 (M012): the finite coordinate set 1,…,n and respectively B_j,T_j; F001 supplies every section's integrability, including the empty-index case. A005 (M013): summands A and −∑T; F001 and BG001–BG002 discharge its premises. A006 (M014): real c=−1 for ∑T and c=2 for A−∑T, with the same compatible structures. A007 (M005): use the total extension of E, Z=ℝ, K=0, and (a,b)=(s,t) or (t,s) in the strict branches; both endpoints are positive, a<b, and evaluate at b∈[a,b]. F004 and BG005–BG008 give differentiability on the interval and within derivative zero on [a,b), including its left endpoint. The bound gives |E(b)−E(a)|≤0. All source conditions are discharged; no E application occurs.",locations:["/inference/applications","/inputs/5/source_contract","/inputs/6/source_contract","/inputs/7/source_contract","/inputs/8/source_contract","/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement","/inference/argument/4/text"]},
argument:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"S001 legitimately specializes N002 at t0>0: t0/2<t0<3t0/2, so its section integrability applies; N004 and N003 give F001. S002 uses integral linearity with the recorded integrability facts and N005 to obtain E'=2∫A−2∑∫T with the correct minus sign. S003 uses M012–M014 to combine these terms. Unfolding the finite-coordinate dot product gives A−∑T=u_t·(u_tt−Δu) by distributivity alone. N001 makes that scalar integrand pointwise zero, and BG004 gives zero integral. S004 generalizes the arbitrary positive time, retaining N002 integrability and N004 differentiability. S005 applies M005 with bound zero on each nondegenerate positive interval, with restriction/locality details checked in BG005–BG008; the reversed and equal endpoint cases give the same equality. No limit exchange, integration by parts or mixed-derivative theorem is newly assumed here: their required conclusions are direct N inputs. Empty spatial or target coordinate sets cause no failure. Every clause of P follows.",locations:["/inference/argument/0/text","/inference/argument/1/text","/inference/argument/2/text","/inference/argument/3/text","/inference/argument/4/text","/definitions/1/definition","/definitions/7/definition","/output/statement"]},
dependency_accounting:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"Each of the nine explicit inputs has an actual role: N001 supplies pointwise cancellation; N002 supplies integrability and the D015 guard; N003 supplies the density derivative identity; N004 supplies differentiation of the actual energy; N005 supplies ∫B_j=−∫T_j; M012, M013 and M014 supply finite-sum, additive and scalar integral linearity; M005 supplies the final interval estimate. Unused clauses within N inputs are not additional premises or unused whole inputs. Omitted routine integrability, zero-integral, locality and derivative-restriction facts are positively checked in BG001–BG008. No original H, absent ancestor, unproved project result or substantive theorem is concealed in a definition or the calculation policy.",locations:["/inference/inputs","/inference/argument","/inference/applications","/definitions/14/requires"]},
dependency_closure:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"Reverse-checked the factual uses in S001–S005. Section integrability, derivative identities, the energy derivative formula, integration-by-parts equality and pointwise cancellation come respectively from direct N002,N003,N004,N005,N001 statements. F001,F002,F003,F004 are proved in order and used only afterward. Dot-product and Laplacian recombination is finite algebra over the explicit D definitions. Integral linearity and the constancy estimate use direct M012–M014 and M005; their routine side facts are precisely BG001–BG008. The controller's elementary-language-4 profile suffices for the remaining order, equality, finite-index and case reasoning; the packet's permission descriptions add no calculus authority. No missing producer body or ancestor statement is used.",locations:["/inference/argument","/inference/inputs","/definitions/1/definition","/definitions/7/definition","/logic/calculations","/foundation_profile"]},
inputs:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"The input port IDs exactly match R006's nine direct inputs. N001 is used only for its explicitly exported orthogonality u_t·(u_tt−Δu)=0. N002 explicitly exports the required scalar-section integrability, with the fixed time lying inside its window; it also directly establishes D015's all-positive-time guard. N003 explicitly exports e_t=2A+2∑B, N004 refers to the actual finite E integral, and N005 explicitly exports the signed integral identity for every spatial coordinate. Instantiations preserve the same u,V,dx,n and positive t0. M005 and M012–M014 are used with real scalar codomains and the displayed finite index set/interval bindings. These are conditional premise checks, not source-fidelity or producer-proof certifications.",locations:["/inputs","/inference/inputs","/inference/argument/0/text","/inference/argument/1/text","/inference/argument/2/text","/inference/applications"]},
object_closure:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"Read every parameter, D001–D015, input/output statement, local declaration, F statement and argument/application. n,m,u,q are global parameters; V,dx and Euclidean coordinate conventions come from D001–D002. D003–D014 give total derivative functions, finite families and scalar products, with no differentiability smuggled into definitions. D015 defines E on positive times and its N002 construction guard is a direct input. t0 is introduced in S001 and generalized by S004; s,t are introduced in S005. x and coordinate indices in integrals/sums are bound, not free witnesses; source dummy variables are locally substituted. N002's C,R,J,Q stay bound in its premise and do not escape the specialization to section integrability. The unnamed total extension in S005 is a legitimate elementary function construction agreeing with E at positive times (arbitrary values elsewhere), and interval/scalar/source objects are locally defined by A007's explicit bindings. No existence, smoothness or structural property is granted merely by an object's name.",locations:["/parameters","/definitions","/inputs","/output","/inference/locals","/inference/argument","/inference/applications/6"]},
output:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"N002 and S004 establish Lebesgue absolute integrability of e(·,r) for every r>0. N004 gives differentiability of the actual E from D015, and S001–S004 establish its derivative is zero at every such r. S005 proves E(s)=E(t) for arbitrary positive s,t in all three order cases. Unfolding D009 and D015 gives exactly ∫V(|u_t|²+∑|u_j|²)dx, with no 1/2 factor. No assertion at zero or negative time is exported, and no hypothesis or normalization is strengthened.",locations:["/output/statement","/definitions/8/definition","/definitions/14/definition","/inference/argument/3/text","/inference/argument/4/text"]},
packet_replay:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"PASS with 8 recorded verified background notes. My mathematical read boundary was the full packet.json (R006, all direct input/output statements, definitions, declarations and prose) plus the logged read-only pinned-Mathlib searches, source reads and successful probes M101–M108 used for BG001–BG008. Declared Yui rules supplied the review procedure. I read no original/project proof, ancestor producer body, prior verdict or other task document, and have no earlier task-proof exposure in this conversation. Conditional on the separately checked carrier/input boundary, this packet plus those positively checked routine notes proves its complete output. This is a mathematical conditional sufficiency judgment, not an attestation of execution isolation, carrier identity, canonical hashing or original source fidelity.",locations:["/inference","/inputs","/definitions","/output","/packet_sha256"]},
scope:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"The arbitrary t0>0 governs S001–S003; S004 legitimately universally generalizes because no extra assumption on t0 was introduced. F004 binds its own positive ρ and is used for arbitrary s,t>0 in S005. The s<t,t<s,s=t branches are exhaustive in the real order, use M005 only in the strict branches, and all yield the same endpoint equality. The extension is local to S005 and its off-domain values never enter the exported conclusion; all evaluated interval points are positive. Input-bound compact sets and box witnesses do not escape. No branch fact, witness or undischarged extra hypothesis is exported.",locations:["/inference/locals","/inference/argument/0/text","/inference/argument/3/text","/inference/argument/3/produces/0/statement","/inference/argument/4/text"]},
statement_closure:{verdict:"PASS",notes:"All consumed content is explicitly stated: N001 gives the scalar cancellation, N002 gives the scalar-section integrability and all-positive-time E guard, N003 gives the precise derivative formula, N004 identifies the derivative of the actual E integral, and N005 gives the signed integral relation. M contracts state their codomain structures, integrability premises and within-derivative interval requirement. D001–D015 resolve measure, dot product, derivative, finite-family and energy meanings. F001–F004 state their exact local conclusions, and P includes all quantifiers, positive-time endpoints and the required normalization. No equation label, original notation or unexported producer representation is needed.",locations:["/inputs","/definitions","/inference/argument/0/produces","/inference/argument/1/produces","/inference/argument/2/produces","/inference/argument/3/produces","/output/statement"]}
};store("checks",checks);
